Negative Beliefs (Examples)
Themselves
I'm constantly falling behind;
I'm no longer capable of doing a good job;
My efforts are pointless;
I've lost all my creativity and skill.
Others
My colleagues/family/team don't notice or care how hard I'm working;
They just keep taking from me and never help;
I can't rely on anyone for genuine support;
My input is ignored or undervalued.
The Future
Nothing will ever change or get better;
This level of stress is going to be permanent;
I'll never find the energy or time to properly recover;
My workload will only increase.
The World / System
The system is fundamentally broken, unfair, or toxic;
It's pointless to fight against the current demands;
The demands are unreasonable and endless;
This is a cynical, zero-sum game.
What Can Cause Burnout
Burnout is rarely caused by individual weakness; rather, it is the result of a prolonged mismatch between the individual and their environment, often stemming from systemic or organisational factors. The primary causes typically include an excessive workload and pace that leaves no time for necessary recovery, reflection, or planning/organising; a fundamental lack of control or autonomy over how, when, or with whom tasks are completed; and insufficient reward or recognition for effort, which quickly depletes motivation and fosters cynicism. Further contributors are a breakdown of the community/social group due to toxic or unsupportive relationships, perceived unfairness in the distribution of tasks or compensation, and a serious conflict between personal values and organisational ethics.  Understanding these environmental and systemic factors is crucial because while burnout can look and feel like simple exhaustion or high stress, it is a fundamen

